<DocsLayout
  title="Viewport"
  subtitle="2D tools emit commands. 3D draws meshes and toolpaths. WP7 is still in progress."
  description="CADCAMFUN viewport: editor2d tools, view3d presets, spatial index, units, and cursors."
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="status" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p>
      WP7 on the task board is <strong>in progress</strong>. A separate session owns
      <code>apps/desktop/src/viewport</code> and <code>src/state</code>. Spec:
      <code>specs/06-desktop-ui.md</code> §2. Spec 06 also says the 3D viewport plays back
      toolpaths and the 2D editor drives the tool state machines. That is the working core.
      It is not the full performance budget (first paint under 1.5 s, no frame over 16 ms on
      the large-drawing fixture).
    </p>
    <DocCallout type="note" title="Read the tool registry and the viewport README together">
      <p>
        <code>editor2d/tools/index.ts</code> registers machines for select, line, polyline,
        rectangle, circle, arc, ellipse, text, dimension, move, rotate, scale, mirror, offset,
        trim, fillet, and chamfer. <code>apps/desktop/src/viewport/README.md</code> still lists
        offset, trim, extend, fillet, chamfer, centre-start-end arcs, rulers, four-view, and
        worker meshing as remaining. Four-view is a <code>ViewPreset</code> in
        <code>view3d.tsx</code>. Worker construction above 50 000 vertices is still described
        as main-thread by that README. There is no material-removal simulation.
      </p>
    </DocCallout>
  </section>

  <section id="paths" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Paths</h2>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Path</th><th>Role</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>apps/desktop/src/viewport/index.ts</code></td><td>Import <code>Editor2D</code> and <code>View3D</code> from here.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/editor2d/editor2d.tsx</code></td><td>Canvas, camera, render loop.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/editor2d/tools/</code></td><td>One state machine per tool. <code>tool.ts</code> defines <code>ToolId</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/editor2d/camera.ts</code>, <code>render.ts</code>, <code>snap-pipeline.ts</code></td><td>Pan and zoom, draw, cursor to snap.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/view3d/view3d.tsx</code></td><td>Three.js scene, orbit, presets, toolpath lines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/view3d/playback.ts</code></td><td>Segments with a <code>rapid</code> flag and durations.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/view3d/toolpath-overlays.ts</code></td><td>Move points, arc helpers, labels. Rapid, cut, arc, and cycle kinds.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/view3d/mesh-jobs.ts</code>, <code>mesh-worker.ts</code></td><td>Mesh build path. The README says current construction runs on the main thread.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/view3d/modeling.ts</code>, <code>viewport-controls.tsx</code></td><td>Solids in the scene, preset picker.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/spatial/grid.ts</code></td><td>Uniform grid index. Items too large for one cell are tracked aside and still returned by every query.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viewport/units.ts</code></td><td>Display conversion. Internal values stay millimetres.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Both views fill the parent. Give the parent a non-zero height. <code>Editor2D</code>
      requires <code>drawing</code>, <code>onChange</code>, and <code>selectedTool</code>. Feed
      emitted drawings back into <code>drawing</code>. External document replacements reset
      history. Echoes of the canvas’s own documents do not. The canvas owns selection and a
      bounded undo stack. Wheel and pinch zoom. Space-drag, middle-drag, and right-drag pan.
      Enter finishes a polyline. Escape cancels a gesture. Delete removes the selection. F fits.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="tools" class="doc-section">
    <h2>2D tools</h2>
    <p>
      Spec 06 §2 lists the tools. Each machine is
      <code>&#123; onPointerDown, onPointerMove, onPointerUp, onKey, preview, command, status &#125;</code>.
      It returns a <code>Command</code>. It does not mutate the document. Shift constrains lines
      and rectangles. A polyline commits once it has two points. Gesture ids do not collide.
      Snapping calls <code>findSnap</code> from <code>@cadcamfun/core</code>.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Tool id</th><th>Machine</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>select</code></td><td><code>tools/select.ts</code>. Click, box, add and remove.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>line</code>, <code>rectangle</code>, <code>circle</code>, <code>ellipse</code></td><td><code>tools/two-point.ts</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>polyline</code>, <code>arc</code></td><td><code>tools/chained.ts</code>. Spec 06 asks for 3-point and centre-start-end arcs. The README still lists centre-start-end as remaining.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>text</code>, <code>dimension</code></td><td><code>tools/annotation-transform.ts</code> via <code>gestureTool</code>. Text starts as the word Text. Type before placing it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>move</code>, <code>rotate</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>mirror</code></td><td>Same gesture module. First point is the origin. Rotation uses drag direction. Scale uses drag length / 10 mm. Mirror uses the drag axis.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>offset</code></td><td><code>tools/offset.ts</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>trim</code></td><td><code>tools/trim.ts</code>. Spec 06 groups trim and extend. There is no separate <code>extend</code> id in <code>ToolId</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fillet</code>, <code>chamfer</code></td><td><code>tools/fillet-chamfer.ts</code>. <code>startTool("chamfer")</code> uses the same factory.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <DocCode language="ts" title="Start a tool">
{`import { startTool } from "./editor2d/tools"

const tool = startTool("line")
tool?.onPointerDown(event)
const command = tool?.command()`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      <code>AVAILABLE_TOOLS</code> is the list of ids that have a factory.
      <code>startTool</code> returns <code>undefined</code> when an id is missing.
      <code>mirror</code> in core reflects about the line through the origin at an angle in
      radians, so a vertical axis is <code>π/2</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="view3d" class="doc-section">
    <h2>3D view</h2>
    <p>
      <code>View3D</code> takes an optional drawing, solids, an extrusion height in millimetres,
      a <code>cam.Program</code>, a preset, and playback props. The caller owns play, pause, and
      the scrub time. Playback stops at the end. Set <code>playbackTime</code> to 0 to restart.
      OrbitControls handles orbit, pan, and zoom. Spec 06’s controls: orbit, pan, zoom, and view
      presets. The scene draws stock as a core box solid, axes, and a grid.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>ViewPreset</code> in <code>view3d.tsx</code> is <code>perspective</code>,
      <code>top</code>, <code>front</code>, <code>right</code>, <code>isometric</code>,
      <code>bottom</code>, <code>back</code>, <code>left</code>, and <code>four-view</code>.
      Orthographic cameras serve the axis-aligned presets. Four-view splits top, front, right,
      and perspective. Orbit is disabled in that preset.
    </p>
    <p>
      Toolpath preview walks the WP2 IR. Rapids are dashed. Cuts are solid. Arcs and helices are
      tessellated. Drilling cycles expand before playback. <code>playback.ts</code> marks each
      segment <code>rapid: boolean</code>. Overlay point kinds are <code>rapid</code>,
      <code>cut</code>, <code>arc</code>, and <code>cycle</code>. The visualizer in
      <code>toolpath-overlays.ts</code> exposes statistics, filterable points, arc-centre
      helpers, and workpiece opacity. Spec 06 excludes material-removal simulation from v6.0.
      The verifier’s height map is a separate CAM panel, not this viewport.
    </p>
    <p>
      Spec 06 asks for mesh buffers off the main thread above 50 000 vertices, using a worker
      and transferable <code>Float32Array</code>s. <code>mesh-worker.ts</code> is in the tree.
      The viewport README says current mesh construction still runs on the main thread, and that
      the large-drawing frame budget is unverified. CADCanvas (owned with WP6) schedules frames
      only while the scene is active: idle scenes schedule nothing, hidden documents suspend,
      and orbit damping runs until the camera settles.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="units" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Units and cursors</h2>
    <p>
      <code>units.ts</code> converts for display. <code>UNIT_TABLE</code> covers millimetres and
      the other display units the status bar offers. <code>toDisplay</code> and
      <code>fromDisplay</code> are the boundary. <code>formatAngle</code> prints degrees from
      radians. <code>programUnitsOf</code> maps inch and thou to G-code <code>inch</code> and
      everything else to <code>mm</code>. <code>DEFAULT_DECIMALS</code> is 3, matching the post.
    </p>
    <p>
      Cursors are SVG files in the docs site at <code>packages/web/public/cursors/</code> and are
      the same set the desktop serves from its public cursors (spec 06: kept in the repo). Files
      present:
    </p>
    <DocCode language="text" title="public/cursors">
{`selected.svg  selected-editable.svg  editable.svg
draw-line.svg  draw-rectangle.svg  draw-circle.svg  draw-arc.svg  draw-polygon.svg
dimension.svg  dimension-edit.svg  text.svg
rotate.svg  rotating.svg  scale.svg  mirror.svg
fillet.svg  chamfer.svg  erase.svg
extrude.svg  revolve.svg  face.svg  edge.svg
drill.svg  mill.svg  cut.svg  toolpath.svg
measure.svg  measuring.svg`}
    </DocCode>
    <p>
      The spatial index in <code>spatial/grid.ts</code> is what keeps snap and hit tests off a
      linear scan. Spec 06’s target is 60 fps with 10 000 entities. The grid test builds and
      queries that count. A stock box that covers many cells is stored beside the grid and
      included in every query.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="boundaries" class="doc-section">
    <h2>Boundaries</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Tools must not mutate the drawing. They emit commands. The editor applies them.</li>
      <li>The viewport must not call Drizzle or the network. Documents and programs arrive as props.</li>
      <li>Three.js stays in <code>view3d</code>. Core geometry stays free of Three.js types. Meshes come from <code>meshOf</code> or from tessellation.</li>
      <li>Display code must not change stored coordinates. Convert with <code>units.ts</code> at the label.</li>
      <li>Toolpath colours distinguish rapids and cuts. They are not a material-removal simulation.</li>
      <li>WP7 must not treat the README’s unfinished list as deleted just because a factory file exists. Confirm the gesture in the tool module before calling a tool done.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
